---
import '@fontsource-variable/roboto-serif';
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ContactForm from '../components/ContactForm.astro';
import { LOCATION_LINE } from '../data/positioning';

const description =
  'For teams whose AI pilot proves something in a notebook and nothing in production: embedded product building that gets a working version running against your real data and your real constraints.';

// No offers or price here on purpose: the rates live on /forward-deployed/.
const serviceSchema = {
  '@type': 'Service',
  '@id': 'https://intentionaut.com/ai-pilot-to-production/#service',
  name: 'From an AI Pilot to a Working Product',
  description,
  provider: { '@id': 'https://intentionaut.com/#person' },
  areaServed: [
    { '@type': 'City', name: 'London' },
    { '@type': 'Country', name: 'United Kingdom' },
  ],
};
---

<BaseLayout
  title="From an AI Pilot to a Working Product | Saielle DaSilva"
  description={description}
  jsonLd={serviceSchema}
>
  <section class="wrap">
    <header class="page-head">
      <p class="eyebrow">Build &amp; delivery</p>
      <h1>Your AI pilot works. It never became a product.</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        For teams with a model that proves something in a notebook and nothing anywhere else.
      </p>
      <p>
        Four to twelve weeks, three days a week, in your repo and against your
        real constraints. You leave with a working path from model output to a
        user decision, the failure modes written down, and a keep, change or
        stop recommendation.
      </p>
      {/* One action above the fold: the form, because it records the arrival
          source (BaseLayout's first_* fields). The booking link sits inside it. */}
      <div class="cta-row">
        <a class="btn-primary" href="#contact-section">Tell me where it stalled</a>
      </div>
    </header>

    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        The pilot did what it was asked to do. The numbers were good enough to
        show people. Then it stopped, somewhere between the notebook and
        anything a customer could touch, and it has been stopped for a while.
      </p>
      <p>
        The usual diagnosis is engineering capacity, and that is usually wrong.
        A pilot answers a question about the model. A product has to answer a
        different set of questions: what happens on the bad input, who is
        accountable when it is confidently wrong, where the data lives, and what the person on the other end is supposed to do with the
        output. Nobody asked those during the pilot, because the pilot was not
        the place to ask them.
      </p>

      <h2>Sound familiar?</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>The demo runs on an export somebody took in March.</li>
        <li>It is accurate enough for a slide and not accurate enough to act on.</li>
        <li>Everyone agrees it should be "in the product" and nobody can say where.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>What is in the way</h2>
      <p>
        Almost always one of three things. The model never met real data, so nobody
        knows what it does with the messy half. The output has no
        decision attached to it, so there is nothing for a user to do next.
        Or the thing it automates was never the expensive part of the job.
      </p>
      <p>
        IDC put numbers on this in the
        {' '}<a href="https://www.lenovo.com/gb/en/explore/lenovo-ai-nomics-idc-cio-playbook-2025-gb">Lenovo CIO Playbook 2025</a>.
        For every forty-one AI proofs of concept, five reached production:
        about 88 per cent never made it. The pilot is not the hard part and
        has not been for a while.
      </p>

      <h2>What I do about it</h2>
      <p>
        I embed with your team for a fixed block and build the smallest real
        version: your data, your systems, a user with a decision to make at the
        end of it. Not a better model. A working path from the thing the model
        knows to the thing somebody does about it.
      </p>
      <p>
        Sometimes that proves the pilot was right and the product around it was
        missing. Sometimes it proves the opposite, and you have saved a
        quarter. Both are useful results and I will tell you which one you got.
      </p>

      <h2>What you get</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>A working path from model output to a decision somebody makes</li>
        <li>An honest read on how it behaves against your real data</li>
        <li>The failure modes written down, including the embarrassing ones</li>
        <li>A build your team can extend or replace on evidence</li>
        <li>A clear answer on whether this belongs in the product at all</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>How it works</h2>
      <p>
        A free preliminary call first, then a block of four to twelve weeks at
        three days a week. The full shape and the rates are on the
        {' '}<a href="/forward-deployed/">product building</a>
        {' '}page. {LOCATION_LINE}
      </p>
      <p>
        If what you need is someone senior to own AI product direction
        over a longer horizon rather than build a proof of concept, that is
        {' '}<a href="/fractional/">fractional and interim leadership</a>
        {' '}instead.
      </p>
    </div>

    <section class="contact-form-section" id="contact-section" aria-label="Get in touch">
      <div class="contact-card">
        <h2>Let's talk.</h2>
        <p class="form-lede">
          If you have a pilot that works and a product that does not exist, tell me where it stalled.
        </p>
        <ContactForm source="ai-pilot-to-production" selected="forward-deployed" booking />
      </div>
      <p class="faq-link">
        Questions first? <a href="/faq/">Read the FAQ.</a>
      </p>
    </section>
  </section>

</BaseLayout>
